cartridge "SKEET/Roger M. Pingleton, Jr./1988"

' SKEET
' A ColecoVision game based on "Skeet" by Roger M. Pingleton, Jr., a GEOS
' desk accessory for the Commodore 64 and 128, COMPUTE!'s Gazette, May
' 1988, page 57 (GEOS column; machine language). Made for Amy Studio by
' Claude (an AI made by Anthropic), 2026, in Amy by Amy Bienvenu.
'
' The game: four PULL balloons sit at the corners of the field. Move the
' arrow onto one and press a button: a clay pigeon flies from a trap
' house, alternately the right one and the left one (right first). Inside
' the field the pointer is a gun sight: press a button to shoot. One shot
' a pigeon, and a button still held from the PULL fires it at once (let go
' first). The pigeon shrinks as it flies away. SCORE counts hits, OUT OF
' throws. GREAT SHOT! (and a spoken "Great shot!"): pull from an UPPER
' balloon and hit the pigeon while it is at its largest (first 5 steps),
' or from a LOWER balloon and hit it at its smallest (after step 25).
' OPTIONS (bottom left, between throws): pigeons 10/20/50/100 (never fewer
' than already pulled) and speed slow/medium/fast. At the end, YOU HIT n%
' in the middle of the field, then PLAY AGAIN or QUIT.
'
' From the original (the article, and orig/skeet.dis, the GEOS program
' disassembled): the four PULL icons and pulling with the pointer;
' sides alternating, right first; the flight in 28 steps of 4,4 (x10),
' 3,3 (x8), 2,1 (x6) and 2,-1 (x4) pixels (x, rise); the five clay
' images 14, 10, 6, 4 and 3 pixels wide (steps 0-4, 5-9, 10-17, 18-23,
' 24-27); one shot a pigeon, read as the button's state at each step (so
' a held button wastes it); a hit is the sight touching the clay (the
' C64's sprite collision), the clay flies on in pieces; GREAT SHOT! for an
' upper icon and a hit in steps 0-4, or a lower icon and a hit after step
' 25 (only a message); options fast/medium (default)/slow and 10/20
' (default)/50/100 pigeons; SCORE: and OUT OF:; YOU HIT n%; play again.
'
' Liberties: joystick pointer instead of the GEOS mouse; the article's
' text breaks off after the first GREAT SHOT case: the lower-icon case is
' read from the code; speeds of 3, 4 and 5 frames a step (the C64 took
' about 1.5, 2 and 2.5: slower here for a joystick); the trapper lets go
' 6-21 frames after PULL at random (the C64 waited a fixed 0.1 s) and each
' throw climbs at one of three angles, so that a fixed routine cannot
' break every pigeon; hit boxes from the clay's size; the options box and
' the end choices are drawn on the field (GEOS menus and icons on the C64);
' left alone 30 s, the end screen goes back to the title; the toy shotgun
' at the bottom (it turns toward the sight, recoils and shows a BANG burst
' on every shot), the field's look, sounds, music and the spoken GREAT
' SHOT are new.
'
' Controls: joystick only (either controller). Stick: move the pointer
' (faster after a moment). A side button: pull on a balloon, shoot in the
' field, choose OPTIONS / PLAY AGAIN / QUIT. Options box: up/down choose,
' left/right or a button change, a button on OK closes it. Title: up/down
' choose, left/right or a button change, a button on START (or HOW TO PLAY,
' CREDITS).
'
' House rules: text in columns 2-29, rows 1-22; tiles from code 128; the
' frame loop is protected from the NMI; screens drawn with the NMI off
' after a wait, display off. Music first in the sound table, effects after;
' the title tune is played once. `set sprite count` after every `clear
' sprites`. Own xorshift random numbers. The end offers PLAY AGAIN / QUIT
' at Amy's request (instead of the usual press-to-title).

